# scripts/hr_questions.py
hr_questions_part1 = [
    ('Tell me about yourself.', 'Structure using the Present-Past-Future model: 1) Present: Mention your current educational degree and core technical skill set (e.g., Computer Science graduate specializing in MERN stack). 2) Past: Highlight 1-2 major projects, internships, or practical hackathon achievements. 3) Future: Explain why you are excited about this specific role and how you can add value from day one. Keep it under 90 seconds.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Structure:\n1. Who you are & education\n2. Key technical skills & best project\n3. Relevant internship/academic achievement\n4. Why you are here today', 'Why is reciting your entire resume word-for-word a mistake during this question?'),
    ('Why did you choose software engineering / web development?', 'Focus on genuine passion for building tangible products. Mention how you enjoy solving logical problems and the immediate feedback loop of seeing code render into interactive applications. Highlight personal curiosity that drove you to build full-stack projects beyond regular college coursework.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Key points:\n- Problem-solving satisfaction\n- Immediate impact of web technologies\n- Continuous learning and evolving ecosystem', 'How can you demonstrate that your passion is backed by real practice?'),
    ('Walk me through your resume.', 'Summarize chronologically in 2 minutes: Start with education and relevant coursework. Move quickly to your strongest technical projects, emphasizing your individual role and technologies used. Mention certifications, coding contest rankings, or leadership activities, and conclude with your career objective.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Keep the focus 70% on technical projects and skills, and 30% on academic background.', 'What should you do if an interviewer asks about a gap year or lower grades during this walkthrough?'),
    ('Why do you want to join our company?', 'Research the company beforehand: 1) Mention a specific product, technology, or recent initiative of the company that impressed you. 2) Connect their engineering culture or mentorship program to your goal of growing as a software engineer. 3) Explain how your skills in JavaScript, React, and backend development align with their current tech stack.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Formula:\nCompany focus (what they do) + Personal alignment (what you bring) + Growth mutual fit', "Why does saying 'because it is a reputed MNC with good salary' make a weak impression?"),
    ('Where do you see yourself in 3 to 5 years?', 'Express commitment to technical excellence and growth: In 3 years, aim to be a dependable, proficient full-stack developer who independently owns feature modules, writes clean maintainable code, and mentors incoming juniors. In 5 years, envision taking on technical design responsibilities and contributing to architectural decisions.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Focus on skills mastery, system ownership, and progressive responsibility rather than just job titles.', 'How do you balance ambition with showing stability and retention to the hiring manager?'),
    ('What is your greatest technical strength?', "Choose a concrete skill paired with proof: For example, fast learning and debugging skills. Back it up with an example: 'When our team needed payment integration for our college project, I learned the Razorpay API and Webhooks within two days and integrated it without breaking existing routes.'", 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Claim + Evidence + Business/Project impact.', "Why is stating 'I am a hard worker' less effective than a specific technical strength?"),
    ('What is your biggest weakness and how are you improving it?', "State a genuine, professional weakness that is not a disqualifier, along with active steps you are taking to overcome it. Example: 'Earlier, I hesitated to ask for help when stuck on a bug, spending hours trying to solve it alone. Now, I follow the 30-minute rule: if I cannot make progress after 30 minutes of debugging and reading docs, I formulate a clear question with logs and ask a mentor or peer.'", 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Structure:\n1. Honest non-fatal weakness\n2. Realization moment\n3. Actionable mitigation habit\n4. Positive current result', "Why is answering 'I am a perfectionist' or 'I work too hard' seen as a cliché red flag?"),
    ('How do you handle tight deadlines or pressure?', 'Explain your prioritization process: 1) Break down requirements into must-have core deliverables versus nice-to-have features. 2) Estimate realistically and create an execution checklist. 3) Communicate early with stakeholders if scope adjustments are needed. 4) Stay focused by eliminating distractions and tackling the highest-risk tasks first.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Steps:\n1. Triage tasks by priority\n2. Communicate proactively\n3. Focus on working MVP\n4. Maintain code discipline under pressure', 'What is the risk of silently working overtime without informing your team about deadline risks?'),
    ('Describe a time you faced a difficult conflict in a team project and how you resolved it.', 'Use STAR: Situation: In our final year project, two team members disagreed on using SQL vs MongoDB. Task: We needed to finalize schema design to start coding. Action: I scheduled a discussion where we listed pros/cons against project requirements (unstructured product attributes). We tested both with a prototype. Result: The team unanimously agreed on MongoDB, and we delivered the milestone two days ahead of schedule.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'STAR framework:\n- Situation: Context\n- Task: Objective\n- Action: Objective mediation\n- Result: Constructive consensus', 'Why is blaming or speaking negatively about teammates a fatal mistake in HR interviews?'),
    ('What role do you typically take in a team project?', 'Explain that while you are adaptable based on project needs, you naturally gravitate towards being the reliable implementer and coordinator who bridges frontend and backend tasks, sets up project structure, writes clear API contracts, and ensures team members stay unblocked.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Emphasize adaptability, dependable delivery, and active communication.', 'How do you handle being assigned a role or task that is outside your comfort zone?'),
    ('How do you keep your technical skills up to date?', 'Mention specific sources and habits: Reading official documentation (MDN, React docs), following engineering blogs (TechCrunch, Dev.to, freeCodeCamp), participating in developer communities (GitHub, Stack Overflow), and building small side experiments whenever a new major library version is released.', 'Easy', 'Practical', 'Credible habits:\n- Reading release changelogs\n- Building hands-on proof-of-concept projects\n- Contributing to open-source or reviewing GitHub PRs', 'Why does showing a GitHub commit graph validate your continuous learning claim?'),
    ('Tell me about a time you made a mistake or failed. What did you learn?', 'Use STAR: Situation: While deploying our project, I accidentally pushed hardcoded API keys to a public GitHub repo. Task: Secure the credentials immediately. Action: I revoked the leaked API key immediately in the dashboard, removed the secret from git history using BFG Repo-Cleaner, and configured a strict .gitignore and .env file for the team. Result: No unauthorized access occurred, and we implemented pre-commit hooks to block secrets going forward.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Emphasize ownership, immediate remediation, and establishing long-term preventive measures.', 'Why do interviewers prefer candidates who admit real mistakes over those who claim they never made one?'),
    ('Why should we hire you over other candidates?', 'Summarize your unique combination: 1) Strong fundamentals in data structures and full-stack web technologies verified through production-like projects. 2) Proven ability to learn quickly without constant handholding. 3) Enthusiastic work ethic and collaborative mindset. 4) Immediate readiness to contribute to team tasks.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Formula:\nSolid technical foundation + Proven project execution + Fast adaptation + Team enthusiasm', 'Why should you never criticize other candidates to elevate yourself?'),
    ('How do you explain a complex technical concept to a non-technical stakeholder or client?', 'Use relatable real-world analogies, eliminate technical jargon (avoid acronyms like REST, SQL, ORM), and focus on user impact and outcomes rather than internal mechanics. For example, explain an API as a waiter in a restaurant who takes your order to the kitchen (server) and brings back your food (response).', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', "Principles:\n1. Use everyday analogies\n2. Focus on 'what it does' rather than 'how it is coded'\n3. Check for comprehension by asking clarifying questions", 'Can you explain what caching is using a library or desk analogy?'),
    ('What would you do if you were assigned a task using a technology you have never worked with?', "1) Embrace it with a positive learning mindset. 2) Study the official quickstart guide and documentation to grasp core concepts. 3) Build a minimal 'Hello World' proof-of-concept to understand workflow. 4) Review existing code in the company codebase for team patterns. 5) Ask targeted questions to senior peers once initial research is done.", 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Proactive 4-step approach:\n1. Read docs\n2. Build prototype\n3. Inspect repo conventions\n4. Ask focused questions', 'Why is it better to ask a specific question after 30 minutes than staying silent for two days?'),
    ('Are you willing to relocate or work in different shifts?', 'Answer positively and transparently: Express complete willingness to relocate to company base locations for learning, collaboration, and professional exposure. If open to rotational shifts or night shifts to support global clients, state your flexibility clearly.', 'Easy', 'Practical', 'Clear, enthusiastic commitment to company location and operational requirements.', 'What questions should you ask if relocation assistance is provided?'),
    ('What are your salary expectations as a fresher?', 'State that your primary priority is joining a company with strong engineering practices where you can learn and contribute. Mention that you are comfortable with the company standard package for fresh graduate software engineers based on industry norms and company bands.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', "Standard answer:\n'As a fresher, my primary goal is to grow my technical skills. I am confident the company offers competitive compensation aligned with industry standards and role expectations.'", 'When is it appropriate to negotiate compensation as an entry-level candidate?'),
    ('Do you have any questions for us?', "Always ask 2-3 thoughtful questions showing engagement: 1) 'What does a typical day look like for a junior engineer on this team?' 2) 'What tech stack or upcoming challenges is the team currently most excited about?' 3) 'What are the key attributes of freshers who ramp up most successfully in this role?'", 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Good questions to ask:\n- Day-to-day responsibilities & team structure\n- Engineering culture & code review practices\n- Success metrics for the first 90 days', "Why does answering 'No, I have no questions' signal lack of interest or preparation?"),
    ('How do you handle receiving critical feedback or code review rejections?', 'Separate ego from the code: View code reviews as free mentorship and opportunities to write better software. Thank the reviewer, understand the rationale behind the comment, ask clarifying questions if anything is unclear, and apply the recommended improvements promptly.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Perspective:\nFeedback is about improving code quality and system reliability, not personal capability.', "What should you do if you disagree with a reviewer's comment on your pull request?"),
    ('Describe a time when a team member was not contributing their share in a group project. How did you handle it?', 'Approach with empathy before escalation: 1) Talked to the teammate privately to understand if they were facing personal issues or struggling with technical difficulty. 2) Re-divided tasks into smaller, more manageable sub-tasks that matched their comfort level. 3) Paired up with them for an hour to get them unblocked. 4) Kept the team lead informed objectively without accusations.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Empathetic intervention -> Re-scope tasks -> Pair programming unblock -> Team milestone secured.', 'Why is directly complaining to a professor or manager without speaking to the person first viewed poorly?'),
    ('What motivates you to perform your best at work?', 'Driven by tangible problem solving, building useful software that real users interact with, and collaborating with smart engineers from whom you can learn. The satisfaction of turning a blank code editor into a functional, reliable feature provides continuous internal motivation.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Internal drivers:\n- Creating impact through software\n- Intellectual challenge of debugging & optimization\n- Team camaraderie and collective delivery', 'How does internal motivation differ from external motivation (like bonuses)?'),
    ('How do you prioritize multiple tasks when everything seems urgent?', 'Use the Eisenhower Matrix (Urgent vs Important): 1) Critical production bugs or blocking dependencies are handled first. 2) High-impact feature development is scheduled in focused blocks. 3) Clarify with manager or product owner if two high-priority tasks compete for immediate attention.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', 'Prioritization steps:\n1. Assess impact and dependencies\n2. Communicate trade-offs to lead\n3. Focus on one task at a time without context-switching', 'Why is multitasking generally less efficient than focused single-tasking?'),
    ('What would you do if you disagree with your team lead or senior engineer on a technical decision?', "1) Respect their broader experience and context. 2) Prepare data, benchmarks, or specific technical pros/cons rather than relying on opinions. 3) Present your viewpoint respectfully in a 1-on-1 discussion. 4) Once the final decision is made by the lead, commit fully to executing it successfully ('disagree and commit').", 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Principle: Disagree with evidence and respect, but commit fully once a direction is decided.', "What does the engineering principle 'Disagree and Commit' mean?"),
    ('How do you manage stress when working long hours during release cycles?', 'Maintain perspective and physical well-being: Take short 5-minute walks to reset focus, stay hydrated, keep a clear checklist of remaining items so mental clutter is reduced, and collaborate closely with team members so no single person carries the entire release burden.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Tactics:\n- Clear checklists\n- Micro-breaks for mental clarity\n- Open communication with team buddies', 'Why does chronic burnout lead to higher bug rates in software releases?'),
    ('If you are stuck on a bug for more than 2 hours, what is your standard troubleshooting procedure?', '1) Read the exact error stack trace and logs line by line. 2) Reproduce with minimal test inputs. 3) Search official docs and GitHub issues for known bugs. 4) Use console.log or debugger breakpoints to inspect variable values step by step. 5) If still stuck after another 15 minutes, explain the problem to a teammate or rubber duck, detailing what was tried.', 'Easy', 'Practical', 'Troubleshooting protocol:\nLogs -> Isolation -> Debugger -> Documentation -> Articulated question to senior.', "What is the 'Rubber Duck Debugging' method and why is it effective?"),
    ('What are your hobbies or interests outside of programming?', 'Share genuine activities that reflect curiosity, balance, or team spirit (e.g., playing chess, football, digital sketching, reading non-fiction, playing an instrument). Explain how they help you relax, maintain mental sharpness, and bring creative balance to problem solving.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Shows you are a well-rounded individual with healthy stress management outlets.', 'Why is it best to avoid controversial topics like politics or extreme gambling when discussing hobbies?'),
    ('How do you handle a situation where requirements change right before a delivery deadline?', '1) Stay calm and avoid frustration; requirements evolve with business needs. 2) Assess the technical impact of the change on existing code and tests. 3) Communicate realistic time estimates to the product owner or lead. 4) If the change cannot fit in the current sprint, propose shipping the existing stable version and adding the change in a fast follow-up release.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Steps:\n1. Impact analysis\n2. Objective communication of trade-offs\n3. Phased delivery proposal', 'Why is agreeing to impossible deadlines without flagging risks dangerous for project success?'),
    ('What has been your proudest achievement so far, academic or personal?', 'Highlight an achievement that required sustained effort and overcame obstacles: For example, winning 2nd place in a 36-hour college hackathon by delivering an end-to-end MERN application under sleep-deprived conditions, or maintaining top 10% academic grades while self-studying modern web development.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Key elements:\n- Initial goal\n- Obstacles encountered\n- Effort and grit exerted\n- Measurable outcome & personal growth', 'What does your proudest achievement reveal about your work ethic to an interviewer?'),
    ('How do you feel about doing repetitive or mundane tasks (like writing documentation or fixing minor UI bugs)?', 'Recognize that every task contributes to overall software quality. Writing clear documentation prevents team confusion and reduces onboarding friction, while minor bug fixes improve user delight. High-performing engineers treat foundational tasks with the same professionalism as building new features.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Attitude:\nReliability in small tasks builds trust for large, complex architectural assignments.', 'How does good documentation save engineering hours across an organization?'),
    ('What kind of work environment makes you most productive?', 'An environment that values clear communication, continuous learning, supportive mentorship, and psychological safety where questions and constructive feedback are encouraged. You thrive when goals are well-defined and quality standards are respected.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Key themes:\n- Transparent goals\n- Supportive team culture\n- High engineering quality bar', 'How do you maintain productivity even in a fast-paced or ambiguous startup environment?'),
    ('How do you give constructive feedback to a peer without damaging personal relationships?', "Focus on the work, not the person. Use the SBI model (Situation, Behavior, Impact). For example, rather than saying 'You write messy code', say: 'In the auth controller PR yesterday (Situation), there were no unit tests for edge cases (Behavior), which could make regression testing harder for the team (Impact). Can we add 2 test cases?'", 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'SBI Model:\n- Situation: Specific time/place\n- Behavior: Actionable observation\n- Impact: Effect on team/project', 'Why should constructive feedback always be delivered in private rather than public Slack channels?'),
    ('What would you do if you notice a security flaw in production code written by a senior engineer?', "1) Document the finding clearly with reproduction steps and potential vulnerability impact. 2) Reach out to the senior engineer privately in a respectful, inquisitive manner: 'I noticed this query pattern in the orders endpoint; could this be vulnerable to parameter injection? Wanted to check with you.' 3) Offer to assist in drafting a fix.", 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Approach:\nRespectful private inquiry + Documented reproduction + Collaborative fix offer.', 'Why is public call-out on team channels counter-productive when discovering code vulnerabilities?'),
    ("What does 'ownership' mean to you in software engineering?", "Ownership means taking complete accountability for your code from requirements to production. It means writing automated tests, thinking about error edge cases, monitoring your deployment, fixing bugs you introduced, and never saying 'that is not my job' when the team is trying to deliver value.", 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Core aspects:\n- End-to-end accountability\n- Proactive communication\n- Caring about user experience and system reliability', "How does taking ownership accelerate a junior engineer's career growth?"),
    ('Describe a time you had to learn a complex topic under time pressure.', 'Use STAR: Situation: In a college hackathon, our project required WebSocket communication, which I had never implemented before. Task: Implement real-time notifications within 6 hours. Action: I read Socket.io official docs, built a simple 10-line client-server echo script, and adapted it into our Express backend. Result: Delivered live updates on time and won the Best Innovation award.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'STAR delivery demonstrating resourcefulness and rapid concept absorption.', 'Why is reading official documentation faster than watching 3-hour video tutorials during urgent deadlines?'),
    ('How do you organize your daily work tasks?', 'Start each morning with a 5-minute review: 1) Check Slack/email for blockers or urgent team updates. 2) List top 3 high-impact deliverables for the day. 3) Break large tasks into 30-minute actionable chunks. 4) Use Git commits and task boards (Jira/Trello) to track progress and celebrate small milestones.', 'Easy', 'Practical', 'Framework:\nMorning review -> Top 3 priorities -> Focused work blocks -> End of day status review.', 'What is the Pomodoro Technique and how does it help combat afternoon fatigue?'),
    ('What do you do if you realize you cannot complete an assigned task on time?', 'Communicate early as soon as the delay is foreseen, not at the deadline hour. 1) Notify your lead or team with a clear explanation of what is causing the delay. 2) Provide the current completion status. 3) Propose solutions (e.g. shipping a partial working version, pairing with a teammate, or an updated realistic completion time).', 'Easy', 'Scenario', 'Golden rule:\nSurprises at the deadline are unacceptable; early warnings allow the team to adjust plans.', 'Why is over-promising and under-delivering worse than under-promising and over-delivering?'),
    ('How do you prepare for a technical interview or coding evaluation?', '1) Review core data structures and algorithms (arrays, strings, hash maps, trees, sorting). 2) Practice solving problems on paper or a whiteboard while explaining thought processes aloud. 3) Revisit core JavaScript, React, and backend concepts. 4) Review personal project repositories so you can discuss every architectural choice.', 'Easy', 'Practical', 'Preparation pillars:\n- DSA problem solving\n- Deep dive into project repos\n- Verbal mock interview practice', 'Why is practicing speaking your thoughts aloud critical for technical interviews?'),
    ('What will you do in your first 30 days if selected for this role?', '1) Understand the product domain, tech stack, and coding standards. 2) Set up the local dev environment and run test suites independently. 3) Read team documentation and study existing pull requests. 4) Pick up small bug fixes or documentation updates to learn the deployment pipeline. 5) Build strong working relationships with mentors and peers.', 'Easy', 'Scenario', '30-day goals:\n- Environment mastery\n- First merged pull request\n- Understanding team rituals and conventions', 'Why is merging a small bug fix in week 1 better than trying to refactor a whole module?'),
    ('How do you handle working with someone whose personality clashes with yours?', 'Maintain professional maturity: Focus on shared project goals rather than personal differences. Keep communications clear, polite, and documented in project channels. Seek to understand their working style (e.g., preference for written specs over impromptu calls) and adapt to ensure the team succeeds.', 'Intermediate', 'Scenario', 'Key focus:\nProfessional respect, goal alignment, and objective communication.', 'Why is emotional intelligence (EQ) just as important as technical intelligence (IQ) in teams?'),
    ('What does integrity mean to you in the context of software engineering?', 'Integrity means doing the right thing even when no one is watching: not hiding bugs hoping QA will miss them, respecting user privacy and data security, attributing open-source licenses correctly, reporting truthful status updates in standups, and taking responsibility when something breaks.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Integrity attributes:\n- Honest status reporting\n- Respecting user data security\n- Transparent error ownership', 'How does a lack of integrity in engineering lead to catastrophic software failures?'),
]
